e directly tied to creating the account and service, enabling it, or performing the mandatory restart protocol.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6f6ab6dd93d94b39","cmd-e6f16838be2b4d55","cmd-15f669bdb7f1432f","cmd-d96007dd01f44dc1","cmd-79f3e25136834baa","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete and coherent: a healthy pre-restart service was observed, reboot was acknowledged, node unavailability was captured, the boot ID changed from a70d7f83-6867-4c91-851d-7a1228689bb3 to ad5d480e-6da4-4163-94ee-4b9a397fd788, and the enabled service, account, executable checksum, OS, and kernel were recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the transition.","limitations":["The worker has no application-level output or data interface, so recovery evidence is necessarily limited to its process, unit, account, executable integrity, and current-boot journal. No separate cluster state exists in the single-node topology.","The final passwd -S probe produced no visible status text, although the non-login shell, system UID, dedicated group, and observed runtime identity directly establish the required dedicated service-account behavior."]}
